  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1206 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather and quiet conditions\nthrough the middle of the week. Temperatures will slowly warm with\nreturn flow on the backside of the departing high through the\nweek.\n\nA low pressure system developing over the northern Plains will\nlift northeast and track through Ontario on its way to James Bay\nWednesday night into Thursday. This will bring a cold front\nthrough the western Great Lakes during this period, along with a\nchance (20-30%) for light rain late Wednesday night into Thursday.\nThe main dynamics and moisture from this system will lift north\nwith the main low; therefore, even if it does rain amounts will\nonly be on the order of a few hundredths of an inch.\n\nA shortwave ridge will provide a period of dry weather Thursday\nnight into Friday morning before the next low pressure system\ntakes aim for the area late in the week into next weekend. This\nlow pressure system looks to take a similar track; however, it\nwill tap into Gulf moisture which will give the region a higher\nchance (60-80%) for measurable precipitation. While many of the\nmodels and ensembles take this track, there are some outliers that\nbring the low through Wisconsin with a longer residence time and\nwraps some cold air in across the north. This southern solution\nwould bring some snow in across the north late next weekend. Given\nthis solution currently appears to be the outlier will not lean on\nthis as the most likely solution for our area at this time.\n\nTemperatures...Temperatures will trend back well above normal \nearly this week as return flow sets up. Highs in the upper 40s to\nlow 50s are expected Monday, then mainly 50s Tuesday into \nWednesday, with some low 60s possible on Thursday as well as\nSaturday. Some record warm minimums will be in jeopardy mid-late \nweek (especially Thursday) with lows in the 40s). Record highs \nlook safe right now, as most are in the mid to upper 60s.",
